description:
What is unclear and for whom: crate-jmap-server/src/lib.rs:571-584 (the panic_message_not_in_response test) checks args["description"].as_str() against the panic string. A future refactor that surfaces panic-payload text through any other field of the error invocation (a custom 'context' field, an 'innerError' nested object, a typed Debug somewhere in the chain) would not be caught by this test.

A future contributor reading the test might believe it provides a general 'panics cannot leak' guarantee, when in fact it only covers one specific surface. The contract documented at the test ('panic payloads may contain secrets, must not be leaked') is broader than what the assertion enforces.

What would make it clear: extend the assertion to recursively walk the entire methodResponse args Value and assert NO field's string serialization contains 'deliberate test panic'. Helper:
    fn value_contains_recursive(v: &Value, needle: &str) -> bool {
        match v {
            Value::String(s) => s.contains(needle),
            Value::Array(arr) => arr.iter().any(|x| value_contains_recursive(x, needle)),
            Value::Object(o) => o.values().any(|x| value_contains_recursive(x, needle)),
            _ => false,
        }
    }
Then the test becomes: assert!(!value_contains_recursive(&args, "deliberate test panic"), ...). One trip through the response tree; catches every future leak path including ones that don't exist today.

Maintenance consequence: low — the current test is correct for the current code. The improved test costs ~10 lines and catches the next refactor that adds a new field.
